RTG write-watch, and a Zorro III ceiling that was zero

Two things in retrodep/mman.c that Picasso96 needs and that the non-JIT build had neither of.

**max_z3fastmem.** main.c's fixup_prefs() holds every Zorro III board to this ceiling - Z3 Fast
RAM, Z3 Chip RAM and the RTG board's VRAM - and cuts anything larger down to it. WinUAE sets it
in preinit_shm() from the size of the address window it reserved, and that whole mechanism is
inside #if defined(NATMEM_OFFSET), which sysdeps.h only defines under JIT. This core has no JIT,
so preinit_shm() never runs and the variable stayed 0: `rtgmem_size > max_z3fastmem` was true of
every Zorro III board ever configured, and the board was reset to 0 before the machine started,
with an error_log nobody reads. The line above it does the same to z3fastmem and the one below
to z3chipmem, so Z3 Fast RAM was being discarded the same way - found while getting the RTG
board to survive fixup_prefs, and fixed with it.

There is nothing to be bounded by here: mapped_malloc() falls back to xcalloc, so the real limit
is the host's memory. 512 MB is the largest size any of the three options offers, so the ceiling
passes everything the front end can ask for and still refuses nonsense. The NATMEM build
overwrites it in preinit_shm() before any machine starts, so it is unchanged there.

**Write-watch.** Picasso96 only converts the parts of the RTG frame buffer the guest wrote since
the last frame. WinUAE answers "which parts" with the kernel: it allocates its memory with
VirtualAlloc(MEM_WRITE_WATCH) and calls GetWriteWatch(). mman_GetWriteWatch()/mman_ResetWatch()
wrapped exactly that, inside #if 0 - and inside the same NATMEM_OFFSET block, so they were doubly
absent.

The honest answer here is therefore "assume every page was written", and that is what this
implementation gives. It is not the waste it looks like:

  * the core hands the front end a complete frame through video_cb() every time round
    retro_run() regardless, so the conversion is the only cost;
  * the alternative is worse than slow. picasso96.c's flush loop does `continue` on a failed
    GetWriteWatch, so an answer of "nothing is dirty" is a black screen, not a stale one.

The signature drops PVOID/SIZE_T/PULONG_PTR for plain types so it compiles without windows.h,
and the two functions move out of the NATMEM_OFFSET block - they have nothing to do with the
natmem reservation and are needed precisely when it is absent.

The page size is fixed at 4096 rather than queried. It only has to be a number both ends agree
on: picasso_allocatewritewatch() sizes its buffer by dividing the board size by it, and this
function fills that buffer. Keeping it constant also means the work an RTG frame costs does not
depend on the host's page size.

Applies to libretro-uae 2b0ed42fe565fb997a0627aaa8f44e0948b527f8 in the order given by
patches/series.txt.

